-- title:
--   Frobenius–Verschiebung factorisation descends to a split unit-root factor
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and let $B$ be a commutative ring carrying a Hopf algebra structure over $\mathbb{F}_p = \mathbb{Z}/p$ whose comultiplication is cocommutative and which is finite and free as an $\mathbb{F}_p$-module. Let $u,\varepsilon,w$ be bialgebra endomorphisms of $B$ with $\varepsilon\circ\varepsilon=\varepsilon$, $\varepsilon\circ u=u\circ\varepsilon$, $\varepsilon\circ w=w=w\circ\varepsilon$, $w\circ(u\circ\varepsilon)=\varepsilon$ and $(u\circ\varepsilon)\circ w=\varepsilon$, so that $u$ is invertible with inverse $w$ on the part cut out by the idempotent $\varepsilon$. Write $B^\vee=\mathrm{CartierDual}\,(\mathbb{F}_p,B)$ for the $\mathbb{F}_p$-dual of $B$ with its Cartier dual Hopf algebra structure, and [`CartierDual.map`](def/HopfAlgebra_CartierDualMap.html#L102) for the transpose of a bialgebra map. Assume the hypothesis `hFV`: for every bialgebra endomorphism $F_k$ of $B$ with $F_k(x)=x^p$ for all $x$ and every $\mathbb{F}_p$-algebra endomorphism $F_D$ of $B^\vee$ with $F_D(\psi)=\psi^p$ for all $\psi$, there are $n\in\mathbb{N}$ and bialgebra endomorphisms $a,b$ of $B^\vee$ such that the $n$-th composition power of the linear map transpose of $u$ equals the convolution product of $F_D\circ a$ and $b\circ(\text{transpose of }F_k)$, the product being taken in the convolution monoid structure on $\mathbb{F}_p$-linear endomorphisms of $B^\vee$ (transported by `WithConv.toConv`/`ofConv`). Let $C$ be a further such Hopf algebra over $\mathbb{F}_p$ (commutative, cocommutative, finite free) and let $q:B\to C$, $i:C\to B$ be bialgebra maps with $q$ surjective, $q\circ i=\mathrm{id}_C$ and $i\circ q=\varepsilon$, i.e. $(C,q,i)$ splits the idempotent $\varepsilon$. The conclusion is that for every bialgebra endomorphism $F_k$ of $C$ with $F_k(x)=x^p$ and every $\mathbb{F}_p$-algebra endomorphism $F_D$ of $C^\vee$ with $F_D(\psi)=\psi^p$ there exist bialgebra endomorphisms $a,b$ of $C^\vee$ such that the linear map transpose of $\mathrm{id}_C$ equals the convolution product of $F_D\circ a$ and $b\circ(\text{transpose of }F_k)$; that is, the same factorisation holds on $C$ with exponent $n=1$ and with the identity in place of a power of $u$.
--
--   This is the slope- or unit-root principle in the form needed here: if some power of the Cartier transpose of $u$ lies in the two-sided ideal generated by Frobenius and Verschiebung, then on the factor cut out by an idempotent on which $u$ is invertible the identity itself admits such a factorisation, which is the ordinarity hypothesis at level one. It is used in the construction of the reduced Cartier dual of a $p$-divisible group, in [`PDivisibleGroup.forall_exists_bijective_tensorProduct_isReduced_cartierDual_of_comp_eq_idempotent_of_reduction_pow_eq_frobenius_conv_verschiebung`](thm.html#PDivisibleGroup.forall_exists_bijective_tensorProduct_isReduced_cartierDual_of_comp_eq_idempotent_of_reduction_pow_eq_frobenius_conv_verschiebung).
